---
import { renderToString } from "katex";
import EvidenceDetails from "./EvidenceDetails.astro";
import JourneyMap from "./JourneyMap.astro";
import LuongOperations from "./LuongOperations.astro";
import LuongResults from "./LuongResults.astro";
import ModeSwitch from "./ModeSwitch.astro";
import { getJourneyOverview, type StageView } from "../lib/site-data";
interface Props { view: StageView }
const { view } = Astro.props;
const { mode } = view;
const expert = mode === "expert";
const journey = getJourneyOverview("encoder-decoder-alignment");
const claim = (id: string) => {
  const e = view.evidence.find((item) => item.claimId === `luong-2015-${id}`);
  if (!e) throw new Error(`Missing Luong evidence: ${id}`);
  return e;
};
const math = (s: string) => renderToString(s, { displayMode: true, output: "htmlAndMathml", throwOnError: true });
---
<article class="stage-shell alignment-stage luong-stage">
  <div class="stage-reading">
    <ModeSwitch activeMode={mode} alternateHref={view.alternateMode.href} />
    <header class="stage-header"><p class="stage-number">Stage 4 of 4 · first preprint August 17, 2015 · EMNLP 2015</p><h1>{expert ? "Audit the state, support and score." : "Where should the decoder look next?"}</h1><p class="stage-question">{view.contribution}</p><dl class="stage-meta"><div><dt>Work</dt><dd>{view.work.title} · Luong, Pham and Manning</dd></div><div><dt>Display source</dt><dd>{view.canonicalVersion.label} · later corrections labelled</dd></div><div><dt>Scope</dt><dd>Recurrent encoder–decoder · global/local source reads · input feeding</dd></div></dl></header>
    <section class="objective-band" aria-labelledby="objective-heading"><h2 id="objective-heading">Your task</h2><p>{view.objective.objective}</p><details><summary>Success criterion</summary><p>{view.objective.successCriterion}</p></details></section>
    <nav class="preset-index mt-4" aria-label="Luong chapter sections"><a href="#luong-timing-heading">Operation order</a><a href="#luong-support-heading">Source support</a><a href="#luong-score-heading">Score families</a><a href="#luong-feeding-heading">Input feeding</a><a href="#luong-results-heading">Paper results</a></nav>
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="luong-timing-heading"><p class="kicker">Architecture · current state before the read</p><h2 id="luong-timing-heading">Update, read, combine, predict.</h2><p>Luong, Pham and Manning retain a stacked-LSTM encoder and decoder. At each target step the decoder first computes its current top-layer state hₜ, using the previous target token and recurrent history. It then reads source states, combines the context with hₜ into an attentional vector h̃ₜ, and predicts a target word.</p><ol class="copy-path" aria-label="Luong current-state operation order"><li><strong>Compute hₜ</strong><span>The recurrent decoder consumes its input and previous recurrent state.</span></li><li><strong>Read cₜ</strong><span>Use hₜ to score source positions and combine their states.</span></li><li><strong>Form h̃ₜ</strong><span>Concatenate the context with hₜ, then apply a learned transformation and tanh.</span></li><li><strong>Predict yₜ</strong><span>A separate target-vocabulary softmax uses h̃ₜ.</span></li></ol><p class="mt-3">Bahdanau’s main-text order reads from the prior state before updating the recurrent state. Luong’s order reads from the current state. The source weights and target-word probabilities still have separate denominators.</p>
      {expert && <div class="equation-panel" role="region" aria-label="Luong global read and target-vocabulary output" tabindex="0"><div set:html={math(String.raw`a_t(s)=\frac{\exp(\operatorname{score}(h_t,\bar h_s))}{\sum_{s'=1}^{S}\exp(\operatorname{score}(h_t,\bar h_{s'}))},\quad c_t=\sum_{s=1}^{S}a_t(s)\bar h_s`)} /><div set:html={math(String.raw`\tilde h_t=\tanh(W_c[c_t;h_t]),\quad p(y_t\mid y_{<t},x)=\operatorname{softmax}(W_s\tilde h_t)`)} /><div set:html={math(String.raw`\mathcal J=-\sum_{(x,y)\in\mathcal D}\sum_t\log p(y_t\mid y_{<t},x)`)} /></div>}
      <p class="mt-3">Training minimizes negative conditional log likelihood over observed target prefixes, including the end symbol. The model remains recurrent in both encoder and decoder. Source-to-target attention here is not Transformer self-attention.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("conditional-nmt-architecture")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("global-attention")} />
    </section>
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="luong-feeding-heading"><p class="kicker">Alignment history · one step later</p><h2 id="luong-feeding-heading">Carry the attentional vector into the next input.</h2><p>Input feeding joins the previous attentional vector h̃ₜ₋₁ with the embedding of the previous target token yₜ₋₁ before the lowest decoder LSTM layer computes hₜ. After reading the source and predicting, the new h̃ₜ can join the following step’s input.</p><ol class="copy-path" aria-label="Input-feeding history path"><li><strong>Previous read and state</strong><span>h̃ₜ₋₁ already combines the previous context and decoder state.</span></li><li><strong>Next recurrent input</strong><span>Concatenate h̃ₜ₋₁ with the target embedding; the first layer receives both.</span></li><li><strong>New decision</strong><span>Compute hₜ, read cₜ, form h̃ₜ and predict yₜ.</span></li></ol><p class="mt-3">The intended benefit is awareness of earlier alignment choices, similar in purpose to translation coverage. This is a learned vector history, not a checklist marking every source word as translated. It does not guarantee against omission or repetition.</p>
      <details class="prediction-reveal mt-4"><summary>{expert ? "Reconstruct the input width and dependency" : "Check which summary reaches the next step"}</summary><p>{expert ? "With n-wide embeddings and attentional vectors, concatenation makes the lowest layer’s input 2n; later layer inputs remain n. hₜ depends on h̃ₜ₋₁, while h̃ₜ is formed only after the current source read. Feeding h̃ₜ into the same step would create the wrong dependency." : "The next step receives the previous attentional vector, which combines a source context with a decoder state. It receives neither a verified word-alignment label nor a set of already-translated words. The current attentional vector is available only after the current read."}</p></details><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("input-feeding")} />
    </section>
  </div>
  <aside class="stage-workbench stage-workbench--long alignment-workbench" aria-label="Static global and local source-operation crosswalk"><LuongOperations mode={mode} /></aside>
  <div class="stage-conclusion luong-conclusion">
    <div class="alignment-evidence">
      <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="luong-score-heading"><p class="kicker">Score family · scalar compatibility</p><h2 id="luong-score-heading">Content and location ask different questions.</h2><p>Dot compares the state vectors directly. General learns a matrix between them. Corrected concat learns a nonlinear scalar from their concatenation. Location derives a fixed-length alignment vector solely from the decoder state; it does not inspect the source states when assigning weights.</p>
        {expert ? <><p class="mt-3">Let dₜ and dₛ be decoder/source widths and k the concat hidden width. The following shapes are dimensional bookkeeping, not reported alternative experimental widths.</p><div class="equation-panel" role="region" aria-label="Luong score-family formulas and corrected concat" tabindex="0"><div set:html={math(String.raw`\operatorname{dot}:\quad h_t^\top\bar h_s\qquad(d_t=d_s)`)} /><div set:html={math(String.raw`\operatorname{general}:\quad h_t^\top W_a\bar h_s\qquad W_a\in\mathbb R^{d_t\times d_s}`)} /><div set:html={math(String.raw`\operatorname{concat}_{\mathrm{v5}}:\quad v_a^\top\tanh(W_a[h_t;\bar h_s])`)} /><div set:html={math(String.raw`W_a\in\mathbb R^{k\times(d_t+d_s)},\quad v_a\in\mathbb R^k`)} /><div set:html={math(String.raw`\operatorname{location}:\quad a_t=\operatorname{softmax}(W_ah_t),\quad W_a\in\mathbb R^{L\times d_t}`)} /></div><details class="prediction-reveal mt-4"><summary>Audit a 3-wide query and 2-wide source</summary><p>The direct dot is undefined. General can map with a 3 × 2 matrix to yield one scalar. Corrected concat can use k × 5 followed by a k-wide output vector. No 1/√d scaling appears in this paper’s dot score. These are shape checks, not numerical attention fixtures.</p></details></> : <details class="prediction-reveal mt-4"><summary>Check whether every score reads source content</summary><p>Location assigns weights using only the decoder state. Dot, general and corrected concat compare it with each source state. A location-based weight can still select source states for the context, but its weighting rule does not compare their content.</p></details>}
        <p class="mt-3">The location vector has fixed length L. The source footnote keeps its initial part for short sentences and ignores late words in longer sentences; it does not specify a new post-truncation normalization rule. Do not turn that exception into an unrestricted full-source guarantee.</p><p class="mt-3">The proceedings’ concat display is Wₐ[hₜ;h̄ₛ], without the complete scalar network. The output vector and tanh appear in arXiv v5’s corrected, unnumbered score display. Its Eq. 8 instead names location; proceedings Eq. 8 names the score family.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("attention-score-family")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("local-attention")} />
      </section>
      <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="luong-history-heading"><p class="kicker">Lineage and versions</p><h2 id="luong-history-heading">Name the inherited mechanism.</h2><p>The stacked-LSTM recipe and source reversal are explicitly linked to Sutskever and the authors’ prior NMT work. The model uses the LSTM unit cited to Zaremba; a shared architecture family does not establish identical cell equations or training details across these papers.</p><p class="mt-3">The global read explicitly extends Bahdanau-style soft alignment while changing timing and state construction. The local branch also names Xu’s soft/hard tradeoff and compares Gregor’s image-generation mechanism. The corpus’s qualified edges carry those documented mechanisms; chapter adjacency supplies no extra edge.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("sutskever-architecture-and-reversal")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("bahdanau-extension")} />
        <dl class="alignment-version-list mt-4"><div><dt>EMNLP proceedings · ten pages</dt><dd>Canonical article, printed pp. 1412–1421. Retains the incomplete concat display; omits dropout probability and D; contains Tables 1–6 without the later alignment appendix.</dd></div><div><dt>arXiv v5 · September 20, 2015 · eleven pages</dt><dd>Corrects concat, qualifies local-p as differentiable almost everywhere, supplies dropout p = 0.2 and D = 10, and adds Appendix A’s Figure 7. Equation numbers diverge after the score display.</dd></div></dl><p class="mt-3">These later corrections remain labelled. The local-p operation ledger preserves unresolved numerical conventions; it supplies no calculated weights, gradient or historical implementation reconstruction.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("version-boundary")} />
      </section>
    </div>
    <LuongResults view={view} />
    <div class="alignment-evidence">
      <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="luong-conditions-heading"><p class="kicker">Experiment envelope</p><h2 id="luong-conditions-heading">Bind the result to its data and recipe.</h2><p>Training uses 4.5 million WMT’14 sentence pairs, with 116 million English and 110 million German words. Pairs longer than 50 words are removed; each language keeps its 50,000 most frequent words and maps others to &lt;unk&gt;. Development uses newstest2013 (3,000 pairs); test sets are newstest2014 (2,737) and newstest2015 (2,169).</p><p class="mt-3">The reported BLEU scores are case-sensitive. Tokenized evaluation uses tokenizer.perl and multi-bleu.perl; NIST evaluation uses mteval-v13a. Metric identity and test year must be retained before subtracting scores.</p><ul class="mt-3"><li>Four LSTM layers of 1,000 cells; 1,000-dimensional embeddings.</li><li>Uniform initialization in [−0.1, 0.1]; plain SGD, learning rate 1; minibatches of 128; gradient-norm threshold 5.</li><li>Ordinary models: ten epochs, with learning-rate halving after epoch five. Dropout models: twelve epochs, with halving after epoch eight.</li><li>Only v5 specifies dropout p = 0.2 and local half-window D = 10.</li><li>MATLAB implementation; reported single Tesla K40 training speed around 1,000 target words per second, with seven to ten days per model.</li></ul><p class="provenance-note mt-3">These are the reported experimental conditions, not universal defaults or an equal-compute comparison. No repeated-seed variance, matched parameter controls or full factorial sweep is supplied.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("data-and-evaluation")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("training-regime")} />
      </section>
      <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="luong-boundary-heading"><p class="kicker">Evidence boundary</p><h2 id="luong-boundary-heading">A useful comparison has a defined reach.</h2><p>Figure 6 groups sentences by length and shows attention systems holding up better than the baseline. It supplies no exact numeric bin table, bucket counts or uncertainty intervals. Its best system changes several ingredients; the trend is descriptive evidence in this test setting.</p><p class="mt-3">The score sweep has missing combinations and unsuccessful runs. Neither its selected rows nor illustrative translations establish universal robustness, a causal ranking of attention mechanisms, or faithful explanations. The paper supplies no repeated-run significance analysis or complete immutable experiment package.</p><p class="mt-3">This journey has moved from fixed source summaries to per-step recurrent source reads, with different state timing, source support and history paths. It has not removed recurrence. Similarity between an unscaled dot score and later attention formulas alone does not establish documented Transformer adoption.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("long-sentence-boundary")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("evidence-boundary")} /><a class="claim-link" href={`/reference/works/${view.work.id}/`}>Inspect the complete seventeen-claim Luong record</a></section>
    </div>
    <nav class="reading-block alignment-progression" aria-label="Alignment journey progression"><p class="kicker">Four chapters · one comparison to carry forward</p><h2>Which information reaches the next prediction?</h2><p>Return to the route map to compare the fixed-summary, prior-state read and current-state read paths. The atlas provides the wider research context, with evidence and causal permissions for each connection.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button" href={`/journeys/encoder-decoder-alignment/${mode}/`}>Return to the {mode} route map</a><a class="button button--quiet" href={`/journeys/encoder-decoder-alignment/${mode}/bahdanau-2014-2015/`}>Return to Bahdanau in {mode} mode</a><a href={`/atlas/${mode}/`}>Explore the {mode} atlas</a></div><div class="mt-4"><JourneyMap journey={journey} currentSlug={view.stage.slug} mode={mode} compact /></div></nav>
  </div>
</article>
